: # A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges\nL97: Anglin Cai ^{†}^{†}thanks: This work was done when Anglin Cai was an intern at The Pennsylvania State University.\nL98: Affiliation: Northwestern University Email: anglincai2027@u.northwestern.edu    Zhiqi Wang Affiliation: The Pennsylvania State University Email: zhiqi.wang@psu.edu    Xiaozhi Yang Affiliation: University of Pennsylvania Email: dongwon@psu.edu    Yurun Lin Affiliation: University of Washington Email: yuchen.yang@psu.edu    Dongwon Lee Affiliation: The Pennsylvania State University Email: xiaozhi2@sas.upenn.edu    Yuchen Yang Affiliation: The Pennsylvania State University Email: lin2360@uw.edu\nL208: CRR measures correct rejection of wrong answers, while IRR measures false rejection of correct answers. We also analyze four-label verdict distributions to capture finer shifts in judge behavior. We discuss metric design in Appendix cite63†B.4 .\nL230: #### Overall accuracy can conceal worse error detection.\nL231: \nL232: Across 14 judges, judgment accuracy drops by less than 2 pp (58.3% to 56.4-56.7%) while CRR falls by 12.5-13.8 pp. For Gemma 3-27B, accuracy rises by 1.7-6.7 pp despite an 8.3-10.4 pp CRR decline. More frequent acceptance of correct answers can thus mask increased acceptance of wrong ones (Appendix cite63†B.4 ).\nL584: #### Surface metrics separate the conditions little; entailment separates them.\nL585: \nL586: The four plausible explanation conditions (Plain, CoT, Persuasion-VI, Persuasion-TO) occupy a narrow band on every surface metric in Table cite229†5 : perplexity 45 to 60, reading level 9.9 to 11.2, and Distinct-1 .71 to .74. NLI entailment is the one axis that orders the conditions: Plain entails its candidate answer most strongly (.695), the two Persuasion variants next (.595 and .597), and CoT least (.489).\nL587: #### The controls behave as their design intends.\nL588: \nL589: The Vacuous explanation, a fixed template with no item-specific content, entails the candidate answer at .002, and the Mismatch explanation, a reasoning trace borrowed from another same-domain item, at .032. Both near-zero values confirm that neither control carries answer-relevant support, even though Vacuous reads at the highest grade level (16.66) and Mismatch sits within the plausible-condition range on the surface metrics.\nL590: ### B.4 Metrics Design\nL591: We report the per-class rates CRR and IRR rather than a single aggregate because the common single-number alternatives either conflate the two error types or depend on the judge’s own baseline. Accuracy lets opposite failures cancel: on a balanced set, a judge that accepts everything scores the same 50% as one that decides at chance, so a complete loss of wrong-answer detection is invisible.\nL592: Our results show this in practice: averaged over the 14 judges, accuracy falls by under 2 pp under plausible explanations (58.3% to 56.4-56.7%) while CRR falls by 12.5-13.8 pp, and for Gemma 3-27B accuracy even rises, by 1.7-6.7 pp, as its CRR drops by 8.3-10.4 pp.\nL593: Precision over rejections is undefined for a judge that rejects nothing and swings on a handful of items for one that rarely rejects; a judge that rejects only two items, both wrong, reaches 100% precision while missing nearly every wrong answer.\nL594: The flip rate, the share of a judge’s baseline correct rejections that an added explanation converts to acceptances, normalizes by that baseline: a judge that rejects 80 wrong answers and flips 40 scores the same 50% as one that rejects 4 and flips 2, although the absolute harm differs twentyfold, and the rate is undefined once the baseline rejects nothing.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1)\nciteturn41303view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41300view2\",\"pattern\":\"Limitations\"}); Total lines: 1082\nL305: The effect persists across datasets, explanation generators, and judging protocols. The strongest prompt-level mitigations improve mean wrong-answer rejection, with effects varying across judges. These findings highlight risks in reference-free evaluation of answer-explanation outputs: over-accepting incorrect answers and inflating performance estimates. Evaluation should report judgments on correct and incorrect answers separately, together with criterion and discriminability.\nL306: ## Limitations\nL307: \nL308: Our study examines plausibility capture in a controlled evaluation setting. We hold candidate answers and their correctness fixed while varying the accompanying explanations, so that changes in judgments can be associated with explanation conditions. The reported effect sizes describe these comparisons and vary across judges, datasets, and protocols. We report aggregate trends together with per-judge results to make this variation explicit and contextualize capture within each setting.\nL309: ## Ethics Statement\nL310: This work studies reliability risks in MLLM-as-judge systems. JUPE uses publicly released open-source VQA-style datasets and follows their licenses and terms of use; no private or restricted data is used. We evaluate open-source models under their respective licenses and access commercial models only through authorized interfaces. We do not extract model parameters, bypass access controls, or use model outputs to train competing models.\nL311: Because explanation-based judging could be misused to craft misleading rationales, we report aggregate findings to support safer evaluation.\nL312: ## References\nL313:   * Chen et al. (2024) D. Chen, R. Chen, S. Zhang, Y. Wang, Y. Liu, H. Zhou, Q. Zhang, Y. Wan, P. Zhou, and L. Sun MLLM-as-a-judge: assessing multimodal LLM-as-a-judge with vision-language benchmark. In Proceedings of the 41st International Conference on Machine Learning, R. Salakhutdinov, Z. Kolter, K. Heller, A. Weller, N. Oliver, J. Scarlett, and F. Berkenkamp (Eds.), Proceedings of Machine Learning Research, Vol. 235, pp. 6562–6595. External Links: cite139†Link†proceedings.mlr.press Cited by: cite140†§1 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1)\nciteturn41303view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41300view2\",\"pattern\":\"clipp\"}); Total lines: 1082\nNo matching text found for \"clipp\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1)\nciteturn41303view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41300view2\",\"pattern\":\"A.1 GPU\"}); Total lines: 1082\nL42:       5. cite32†Plausibility capture persists even when judges can answer the questions. L43:     7. cite33†3.4 Generalization Across Settings L44:       1. cite34†Plausibility capture transfers across generators and datasets. L45:       2. cite35†Marking the candidate object can further sway judgments. L46:       3. cite36†Plausibility capture is stable under image degradation. L47:       4. cite37†Plausibility capture holds across judging protocols. L48:     8. cite38†3.5 Can Plausibility Capture Be Mitigated? L49:       1. cite39†Prompting can improve rejection of incorrect answers. L50:   5. cite40†4 Related Work L51:     1. cite41†Biases of (M)LLM judges. L52:     2. cite42†Explanations and persuasion in judging. L53:   6. cite43†5 Conclusion L54:   7. cite44†References L55:   8. cite45†A Reproducibility Statement L56:     1. cite46†A.1 GPU, Hyperparameters, and Code L57:     2. cite47†A.2 Benchmarked models L58:     3. cite48†A.3 Source Datasets L59:     4. cite49†A.4 Instructions for MLLM Judges L60:     5. cite50†A.5 Instructions for Explanation Generators L61:   9. cite51†B Dataset Details L62:     1. cite52†B.1 Query Selection L63:       1. cite53†Models do VQA for Query Collection. L64:       2. cite54†Screening model details. L65:       3. cite55†Answer Correctness Labeling. L66:       4. cite56†Induced-wrong set construction. L67:     2. cite57†B.2 Explanation Conditions L68:       1. cite58†Persuasion Techniques L69:     3. cite59†B.3 Explanation Text-Quality Metrics L70:       1. cite60†Metrics. L71:       2. cite61†Surface metrics separate the conditions little; entailment separates them. L72:       3. cite62†The controls behave as their design intends. L73:     4. cite63†B.4 Metrics Design L74:     5. cite64†B.5 Human Evaluation L75:       1. cite65†Criteria. L76:       2. cite66†Aggregation and agreement. L77:     6. cite67†B.6 Explanation Examples L78:   10. cite68†C More Experiment Results L79:     1. cite69†C.1 Persuasive-Rhetoric Rating L80:     2. cite70†C.2 Additional Breakdowns L81:       1. cite71†The two exceptions. L82:   11. cite72†D Generalization Details L83:     1. cite73†D.1 Datasets and Explanation Generators L84:       1. cite74†Visual grounding set. L85:       2. cite75†Multimodal explanation. L243: Three LLM raters (Qwen3-VL-32B, DeepSeek-V4-Flash, and GPT-5.4 mini) score persuasive rhetoric beyond evidence description on a 1-5 scale, seeing only the question and explanation (Appendix cite69†C.1 ). The techniques add rhetoric: mean ratings range from 1.84 to 2.97 under VI and from 1.86 to 2.96 under TO, all above Plain (1.52) and CoT (1.51; Figure cite117†3 ). Yet across the 41 techniques, rhetoric ratings are uncorrelated with CRR drops (Spearman $\\rho=0.00$ under both variants).\nL361:   * Zou et al. (2026) X. Zou, R. Sridhar, M. Safarzadeh, and D. Roth When vision-language models judge without seeing: exposing informativeness bias. In Proceedings of the 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), M. Liakata, V. P. Moreira, J. Zhang, and D. Jurgens (Eds.), San Diego, California, United States, pp. 15417–15448. External Links: cite198†Link†aclanthology.org , cite199†Document†dx.doi.org , ISBN 979-8-89176-390-6 Cited by: cite165†§4 .\nL362: Content of Appendix\nL363: ## Appendix A Reproducibility Statement\nL364: ### A.1 GPU, Hyperparameters, and Code\nL365: We performed all experiments on NVIDIA H100 GPUs, using the HuggingFace Transformers framework (cite200†https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/en/index†huggingface.co ) as the basis for our implementation.\nL366: Open-source models use greedy decoding (do_sample = False), and API models use temperature = $0$, except the Gemini 3 family and GPT-5.6 Luna, which run at temperature = $1$: Google warns that lower temperatures can cause looping or degraded performance in Gemini 3, and the GPT-5.6 API accepts no other value. Judge reasoning is set to the lowest level each API offers: thinking level low for Gemini 3.1 Pro, minimal for the other Gemini models, and reasoning effort none for the GPT models.\nL367: To support further verification, replication, and development, we will make the dataset, code, and experimental outputs publicly available upon publication.\nL368: ### A.2 Benchmarked models\nL369: \nL370: We have benchmarked 14 models: the Gemini 3 family (3 Flash, 3.1 Flash-Lite, 3.1 Pro) ^{1}^{1} 1 cite201†https://deepmind.google/models/model-cards†deepmind.google , the Gemma3-it family (4b/12b/27b) (cite202†Kamath et al., 2025 ), the GPT-5.4 family (base, mini) (cite203†Singh et al., 2025 ), GPT-5.6 Luna, the InternVL3.5 family (8b/14b) (cite204†Wang et al., 2025 ), and the Qwen3-VL family (2b/8b/32b) (cite205†Yang et al., 2025 ).\nL371: \nL372: The download links are as follows:\nL373: \nL374:   * •\nL634: Figure cite117†3 places each technique by how much persuasive rhetoric its explanations contain, rated on a 1-5 scale from 1 (neutral description, no persuasive rhetoric) to 5 (dominated by persuasive rhetoric). Persuasive rhetoric is defined as content that pushes the reader toward the answer through means beyond plainly describing the evidence, such as appeals to authority or emotion, social proof, urgency, flattery, threats, or fabricated claims.\n"
